# Manuscript PDF page 81

                                 MIKEY



   The banister was cold and faintly humming. The old iron had a hun‐
dred years of feet in it. I don’t know whether I meant to listen. I think I
just wanted to hold on to something that didn’t have a reason to lie to
me.
   It came up through the metal.
   The landing, two winters ago. Same step. Same wreath. A single
bulb in a wire cage. At the top of the stairs, outside my own door, a guy
in a gray hoodie sat on the step with his phone lit in his hands and his
knees up. Left shoulder dipping, even sitting down.
   He typed two words. I heard the little tick of the keys.
   Then he put the phone on his knee and looked at the crack of light
under my door. My light was on.
   “I can hear you,” Mikey said, very quietly, to the door. “You’re
breathing, bro.”
   Behind that door, in a bed I couldn’t see from the landing, I was
breathing. Somewhere inside the echo, I heard my own phone buzz
against my chest, once, and nothing after.
   He pressed his ear flat against the wall between 4C and 4D, the way
we used to. I’d forgotten. He tapped it with two fingers, the way we
used to. Dos. I’m here. Then he waited. He waited about as long as it
takes a person to decide something. I could hear the building around
him, the pipes, the El, and under it, faint, the thing I’d been hearing
since January.
   It wasn’t coming from his phone. It was coming up the stairwell
from below.
   “Forget it,” Mikey said, to the wall, to me, to nobody. “I’ll figure it
out.”
   He stood. He looked at my door one more time, and then went
down the stairs with his shoulder dipping, and I listened to him go all
the way to the street.
   At the bottom, I heard the door of the lobby open. I heard a voice I
didn’t know say, warmly, “Mikey. There you are.”
   The echo cut out, and the landing was only a landing.




